(window.RW_DISPLAY = window.RW_DISPLAY || {})["test10_1A"] = [{"id":"5d2fd27d","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Words in Context","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Craft and Structure · Words in Context","passage":"While we can infer information about climate activity in Earth’s distant past from physical evidence, we of course cannot observe past climates directly. To study early Earth’s climate in action, we must ______ that climate using computer models that represent various climate conditions consistent with the physical evidence.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"While we can infer information about climate activity in Earth’s distant past from physical evidence, we of course cannot observe past climates","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"directly. To study early Earth’s climate in action, we must ______ that climate using computer models that represent various climate conditions","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"consistent with the physical evidence.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["invent","simulate","exaggerate","preserve"]},{"id":"ac5bf490","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Craft and Structure · Text Structure and Purpose","passage":"The following text is from Reyna Grande’s 2012 memoir The Distance Between Us. In the text, Grande reflects on a time when she and her older sister Mago were children. A Barbie is a type of doll. I begged Mago to come play with me. She wasn’t as interested in my Barbie as she had been just two months before, which made me sad because that was the only thing Mago had envied me for. Usually, it was me doing the envying. After I pestered her relentlessly, she finally put her notebook down and came over to me. ©2012 by Reyna Grande","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"The following text is from Reyna Grande’s 2012 memoir The Distance Between Us. In the text, Grande reflects on a time when she and her older","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"sister Mago were children. A Barbie is a type of doll.","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"I begged Mago to come play with me. She wasn’t as interested in my Barbie as she had been just two months before, which made","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"me sad because that was the only thing Mago had envied me for. Usually, it was me doing the envying. After I pestered her","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"relentlessly, she finally put her notebook down and came over to me.","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"©2012 by Reyna Grande","indent":4}],"question":"Which choice best describes the function of the underlined portion in the text?","choices":["It indicates Grande’s unhappiness over Mago’s disinterest in Grande’s doll.","It expresses Grande’s anger over Mago’s refusal to spend time with her.","It presents an example of how Mago and Grande generally played together as children.","It provides a reason for why Mago is unwilling to play with Grande."],"underline":null,"underline_segments":["which made","me sad because that was the only thing Mago had envied me for."]},{"id":"af43b0bd","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Craft and Structure · Text Structure and Purpose","passage":"Researchers have long hypothesized that woolly mammoths were hunted to extinction in North America by humans using spears with grooved tips known as Clovis points. One anthropologist set out to test this hypothesis. Using a mechanical spear-thrower, he launched spears with Clovis points into mounds of clay — substitutes for the animals large bodies. The projectiles generally penetrated only a few inches into the clay, an amount insufficient to have harmed most woolly mammoths. This led the anthropologist to conclude that hunters using spears with Clovis points likely weren’t the principal drivers of the extinction.","question":"Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?","choices":["To argue for the significance of new findings amid an ongoing debate among researchers","To discuss the advantages and disadvantages of the method used in an experiment","To summarize two competing hypotheses and a major finding associated with each one","To describe an experiment whose results cast doubt on an established hypothesis"]},{"id":"f52cc78c","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Cross-Text Connections","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Craft and Structure · Cross-Text Connections","passage":"Text 1 Polar bears sustain themselves primarily by hunting seals on the Arctic sea ice, but rising ocean temperatures are causing the ice to diminish, raising concerns about polar bear population declines as these large predators’ seal-hunting habitats continue to shrink. A 2020 study examining polar bear populations across the Arctic concluded that populations affected by sea-ice loss are at great risk of extinction by the end of the twenty-first century. Text 2 Monitoring carried out by researchers from the Norwegian Polar Institute shows that the polar bear population on the Arctic archipelago of Svalbard remains stable and well nourished despite rapidly declining sea ice in recent years. The researchers attribute this population’s resilience in part to a shift in feeding strategies: in addition to hunting seals, the Svalbard polar bears have begun relying on a diet of reindeer meat and birds’ eggs.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Text 1","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Polar bears sustain themselves primarily by hunting seals on the Arctic sea ice, but rising ocean temperatures are causing the ice to diminish,","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"raising concerns about polar bear population declines as these large predators’ seal-hunting habitats continue to shrink. A 2020 study examining","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"polar bear populations across the Arctic concluded that populations affected by sea-ice loss are at great risk of extinction by the end of the","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"twenty-first century.","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Text 2","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Monitoring carried out by researchers from the Norwegian Polar Institute shows that the polar bear population on the Arctic archipelago of","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Svalbard remains stable and well nourished despite rapidly declining sea ice in recent years. The researchers attribute this population’s resilience","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"in part to a shift in feeding strategies: in addition to hunting seals, the Svalbard polar bears have begun relying on a diet of reindeer meat and","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"birds’ eggs.","indent":0}],"question":"Based on the texts, how would the researchers in Text 2 most likely respond to the conclusion presented in the underlined portion of Text 1?","choices":["By noting that it neglects the possibility of some polar bear populations adapting to changes in their environment","By suggesting that it is likely incorrect about the rates at which warming ocean temperatures have caused sea ice to melt in the Arctic","By asserting that it overlooks polar bear populations that have not yet been affected by loss of seal-hunting habitats","By arguing that it fails to account for polar bears’ reliance on a single seal-hunting strategy"],"underline":null,"underline_segments":["concluded that populations affected by sea-ice loss are at great risk of extinction by the end of the","twenty-first century."]},{"id":"47598085","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Craft and Structure · Text Structure and Purpose","passage":"Yawn contagion occurs when one individual yawns in response to another’s yawn. Studies of this behavior in primates have focused on populations in captivity, but biologist Elisabetta Palagi and her colleagues have shown that it can occur in wild primate populations as well. In their study, which focused on a wild population of gelada monkeys (Theropithecus gelada) in Ethiopia, the researchers further reported that yawn contagion most commonly occurred in males and across different social groups instead of within a single social group.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Yawn contagion occurs when one individual yawns in response to another’s yawn. Studies of this behavior in primates have focused on","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"populations in captivity, but biologist Elisabetta Palagi and her colleagues have shown that it can occur in wild primate populations as well. In","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"their study, which focused on a wild population of gelada monkeys (Theropithecus gelada) in Ethiopia, the researchers further reported that yawn","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"contagion most commonly occurred in males and across different social groups instead of within a single social group.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice best describes the function of the first sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It defines a phenomenon that is discussed in the text.","It introduces a problem that is examined in the text.","It makes a claim that is challenged in the text.","It presents a hypothesis that is evaluated in the text."],"underline":"Yawn contagion occurs when one individual yawns in response to anothers ’ yawn.","underline_segments":["Yawn contagion occurs when one individual yawns in response to another’s yawn."]},{"id":"ca50de52","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Craft and Structure · Text Structure and Purpose","passage":"“How lifelike are they?” Many computer animators prioritize this question as they strive to create ever more realistic environments and lighting. Generally, while characters in computer-animated films appear highly exaggerated, environments and lighting are carefully engineered to mimic reality. But some animators, such as Pixar’s Sanjay Patel, are focused on a different question. Rather than asking first whether the environments and lighting they’re creating are convincingly lifelike, Patel and others are asking whether these elements reflect their films’ unique stories.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"“How lifelike are they?” Many computer animators prioritize this question as they strive to create ever more realistic environments and lighting.","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Generally, while characters in computer-animated films appear highly exaggerated, environments and lighting are carefully engineered to mimic","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"reality. But some animators, such as Pixar’s Sanjay Patel, are focused on a different question. Rather than asking first whether the environments","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"and lighting they’re creating are convincingly lifelike, Patel and others are asking whether these elements reflect their films’ unique stories.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice best describes the function of the underlined question in the text as a whole?","choices":["It reflects a primary goal that many computer animators have for certain components of the animations they produce.","It represents a concern of computer animators who are more interested in creating unique backgrounds and lighting effects than realistic ones.","It conveys the uncertainty among many computer animators about how to create realistic animations using current technology.","It illustrates a reaction that audiences typically have to the appearance of characters created by computer animators."],"underline":"“How lifelike are they?”","underline_segments":["“How lifelike are they?”"]},{"id":"34d7bb25","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Craft and Structure · Text Structure and Purpose","passage":"According to Indian economist and sociologist Radhakamal Mukerjee (1889 – 1968), the Eurocentric concepts that informed early twentieth- century social scientific methods —for example, the idea that all social relations are reducible to struggles between individuals — had little relevance for India. Making the social sciences more responsive to Indians needs, Mukerjee argued, required constructing analytical categories informed by India’s cultural and ecological circumstances. Mukerjee thus proposed the communalist Indian village as the ideal model on which to base Indian economic and social policy.","question":"Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?","choices":["The text recounts Mukerjee’s early training in the social scientific disciplines and then lists social policies whose implementation Mukerjee oversaw.","The text mentions some of Mukerjee’s economic theories and then traces their impact on other Indian social scientists of the twentieth century.","The text presents Mukerjee’s critique of the social sciences and then provides an example of his attempts to address issues he identified in his critique.","The text explains an influential economic theory and then demonstrates how that theory was more important to Mukerjee’s work than other social scientists have acknowledged."]},{"id":"c4900368","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Craft and Structure · Text Structure and Purpose","passage":"The following text is from the 1924 poem “Cycle” by D Arcy McNickle, who was a citizen of the Confederated Salish and Kootenai Tribes. There shall be new roads wending, A new beating of the drum — Men’s eyes shall have fresh seeing, Grey lives reprise their span — But under the new sun’s being, Completing what night began, There ll be the same backs bending, The same sad feet shall drum — When this night finds its ending And day shall have come.....","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"The following text is from the 1924 poem “Cycle” by D’Arcy McNickle, who was a citizen of the Confederated Salish and Kootenai Tribes.","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"There shall be new roads wending,","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"A new beating of the drum—","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Men’s eyes shall have fresh seeing,","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Grey lives reprise their span—","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"But under the new sun’s being,","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Completing what night began,","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"There’ll be the same backs bending,","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"The same sad feet shall drum—","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"When this night finds its ending","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"And day shall have come.....","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?","choices":["To consider how the repetitiveness inherent in human life can be both rewarding and challenging","To question whether activities completed at one time of day are more memorable than those completed at another time of day","To refute the idea that joy is a more commonly experienced emotion than sadness is","To demonstrate how the experiences of individuals relate to the experiences of their communities"]},{"id":"eb775f90","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Command of Evidence","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Information and Ideas · Command of Evidence","passage":"Annual Car Production in the United States, 1910 – 1925 Year Number of cars produced Number of companies producing cars 1910 123,990 320 1915 548,139 224 1920 1,651,625 197 1925 3,185,881 80 A student is using the table as part of a social studies class presentation on the US auto industry in the early twentieth century. The student notes that, according to the table, from 1910 to 1925 ______","passage_lines":[{"type":"table","title":"Annual Car Production in the United States, 1910–1925","headers":["Year","Number of cars produced","Number of companies producing cars"],"rows":[["1910","123,990","320"],["1915","548,139","224"],["1920","1,651,625","197"],["1925","3,185,881","80"]]},{"type":"text","text":"A student is using the table as part of a social studies class presentation on the US auto industry in the early twentieth century. The student notes that, according to the table, from 1910 to 1925 ______"}],"question":"Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the statement?","choices":["the number of cars produced increased but the number of companies producing cars decreased.","both the number of cars produced and the number of companies producing cars remained unchanged.","the number of cars produced decreased but the number of companies producing cars remained unchanged.","both the number of cars produced and the number of companies producing cars increased."],"underline":null,"underline_segments":["Annual Car Production in the","the United States, 1910–1925"]},{"id":"458b4a11","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Central Ideas and Details","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Information and Ideas · Central Ideas and Details","passage":"To understand how temperature change affects microorganism-mediated cycling of soil nutrients in alpine ecosystems, Eva Ka štovská et al. collected plant-soil cores in the Tatra Mountains at elevations around 2,100 meters and transplanted them to elevations of 1,700 –1,800 meters, where the mean air temperature was warmer by 2°C. Microorganism-mediated nutrient cycling was accelerated in the transplanted cores; crucially, microorganism community composition was unchanged, allowing Ka štovská et al. to attribute the acceleration to temperature-induced increases in microorganism activity.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"To understand how temperature change affects microorganism-mediated cycling of soil nutrients in alpine ecosystems, Eva Kaštovská et al.","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"collected plant-soil cores in the Tatra Mountains at elevations around 2,100 meters and transplanted them to elevations of 1,700–1,800 meters,","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"where the mean air temperature was warmer by 2°C. Microorganism-mediated nutrient cycling was accelerated in the transplanted cores;","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"crucially, microorganism community composition was unchanged, allowing Kaštovská et al. to attribute the acceleration to temperature-induced","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"increases in microorganism activity.","indent":0},{"type":"blank"}],"question":"It can most reasonably be inferred from the text that the finding about the microorganism community composition was important for which reason?","choices":["It provided preliminary evidence that microorganism-mediated nutrient cycling was accelerated in the transplanted cores.","It suggested that temperature-induced changes in microorganism activity may be occurring at increasingly high elevations.","It ruled out a potential alternative explanation for the acceleration in microorganism-mediated nutrient cycling.","It clarified that microorganism activity levels in the plant-soil cores varied depending on which microorganisms comprised the community."]},{"id":"69d662af","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Central Ideas and Details","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Information and Ideas · Central Ideas and Details","passage":"The following text is adapted from Ann Petry’s 1946 novel The Street. Lutie lives in an apartment in Harlem, New York. The glow from the sunset was making the street radiant. The street is nice in this light, [Lutie] thought. It was swarming with children who were playing ball and darting back and forth across the sidewalk in complicated games of tag. Girls were skipping double dutch rope, going tirelessly through the exact center of a pair of ropes, jumping first on one foot and then the other. ©1946 by Ann Petry","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"The following text is adapted from Ann Petry’s 1946 novel The Street. Lutie lives in an apartment in Harlem, New York.","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"The glow from the sunset was making the street radiant. The street is nice in this light, [Lutie] thought. It was swarming with","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"children who were playing ball and darting back and forth across the sidewalk in complicated games of tag. Girls were skipping","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"double dutch rope, going tirelessly through the exact center of a pair of ropes, jumping first on one foot and then the other.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"©1946 by Ann Petry","indent":4}],"question":"Which choice best describes what is happening in the text?","choices":["Lutie is observing the appearance of the street at a particular time of day and the events occurring on it.","Lutie is annoyed by the noise of children playing games on her street.","Lutie is puzzled by the rules of certain children’s games.","Lutie is spending time alone in her apartment because she doesn’t want to interact with her neighbors."]},{"id":"ac7166f7","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Command of Evidence","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Information and Ideas · Command of Evidence","passage":"Researchers Narelle Haworth and Amy Schramm studied bicycling behavior in Queensland, Australia. Haworth and Schramm asked adult bike riders questions about their level of experience, reasons for riding a bike, and route preferences. The researchers claim that experienced riders who mainly bike to work tend to prefer routes that reduce their travel time.","question":"Which survey response from a bike rider in Queensland would best support the underlined claim?","choices":["“I have a bike, but I don’t ride it very often. When the weather is nice, I sometimes use my bike to go into town to do some shopping.”","“I just got a new bike, and I’m looking forward to going on rides with my friends soon.”","“I bike to my job every day. There’s a nice bike path that goes through a park, but I use the bike lane on the main road because it’s faster.”","“I usually bike to work, but I’m taking the bus now because my bike has a broken part that needs to be fixed.”"],"underline":null,"underline_segments":["experienced riders","who mainly bike to work tend to prefer routes that reduce their travel time."]},{"id":"3a1f02b0","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Central Ideas and Details","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Information and Ideas · Central Ideas and Details","passage":"The following text is adapted from Frances Hodgson Burnett’s 1911 novel The Secret Garden. Mary, a young girl, recently found an overgrown hidden garden. Mary was an odd, determined little person, and now she had something interesting to be determined about, she was very much absorbed, indeed. She worked and dug and pulled up weeds steadily, only becoming more pleased with her work every hour instead of tiring of it. It seemed to her like a fascinating sort of play.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"The following text is adapted from Frances Hodgson Burnett’s 1911 novel The Secret Garden. Mary, a young girl, recently found an overgrown","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"hidden garden.","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Mary was an odd, determined little person, and now she had something interesting to be determined about, she was very much absorbed,","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"indeed. She worked and dug and pulled up weeds steadily, only becoming more pleased with her work every hour instead of tiring of it. It","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"seemed to her like a fascinating sort of play.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Mary hides in the garden to avoid doing her chores.","Mary is getting bored with pulling up so many weeds in the garden.","Mary is clearing out the garden to create a space to play.","Mary feels very satisfied when she’s taking care of the garden."]},{"id":"1703403b","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Command of Evidence","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Information and Ideas · Command of Evidence","passage":"Average Monthly Rainfall in Select Puerto Rican Cities from 1981 to 2010 14 12 Average rainfall (inches) 10 8 6 4 2 0 ay ly t r ne Ju us be M Ju g Au em ept S Month Rincón Isabela San Sebastián Ponce A student is presenting average monthly rainfall totals in various Puerto Rican cities for a science class. During the presentation, the student notes that in September ______","passage_lines":[{"type":"chart","note":"[Graph: Average Monthly Rainfall in Select Puerto Rican Cities, 1981–2010 — not available here]"},{"type":"text","text":"A student is presenting average monthly rainfall totals in various Puerto Rican cities for a science class. During the presentation, the student notes that in September ______"}],"question":"Which choice most effectively uses data from the graph to complete the statement?","choices":["Rincón s average rainfall is greater than that of Isabela, San Sebastián, and Ponce.","Rincón and Ponce have an average rainfall of about 5 inches, and Isabela and San Sebastián have an average rainfall of about 10 inches.","Rincón, Ponce, and Isabela each have an average rainfall below 8 inches, but San Sebastián s average rainfall that month is greater than 8 inches.",""],"unusable":"missing graph"},{"id":"87aa7bab","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Central Ideas and Details","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Information and Ideas · Central Ideas and Details","passage":"A common assumption among art historians is that the invention of photography in the mid-nineteenth century displaced the painted portrait in the public consciousness. The diminishing popularity of the portrait miniature, which coincided with the rise of photography, seems to support this claim. However, photography’s impact on the portrait miniature may be overstated. Although records from art exhibitions in the Netherlands from 1820 to 1892 show a decrease in the number of both full-sized and miniature portraits submitted, this trend was established before the invention of photography. Based on the text,","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"A common assumption among art historians is that the invention of photography in the mid-nineteenth century displaced the painted portrait in","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"the public consciousness. The diminishing popularity of the portrait miniature, which coincided with the rise of photography, seems to support","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"this claim. However, photography’s impact on the portrait miniature may be overstated. Although records from art exhibitions in the Netherlands","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"from 1820 to 1892 show a decrease in the number of both full-sized and miniature portraits submitted, this trend was established before the","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"invention of photography.","indent":0}],"question":"Based on the text, what can be concluded about the diminishing popularity of the portrait miniature in the nineteenth century?","choices":["Factors other than the rise of photography may be more directly responsible for the portrait miniature’s decline.","Although portrait miniatures became less common than photographs, they were widely regarded as having more artistic merit.","The popularity of the portrait miniature likely persisted for longer than art historians have assumed.","As demand for portrait miniatures decreased, portrait artists likely shifted their creative focus to photography."]},{"id":"684b8bd2","domain":"Standard English","skill":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Standard English · Form, Structure, and Sense","passage":"Far from being modern inventions, ______ more than 5,000 years ago.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Far from being modern inventions, ______ more than 5,000 years ago.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["Sumerians in ancient Mesopotamia used drinking straws","drinking straws were used by Sumerians in ancient Mesopotamia","the use of drinking straws by Sumerians in ancient Mesopotamia happened","ancient Mesopotamia was home to Sumerians who used drinking straws"]},{"id":"1b97cce9","domain":"Standard English","skill":"Boundaries","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Standard English · Boundaries","passage":"Hegra is an archaeological site in present-day Saudi Arabia and was the second largest city of the Nabataean Kingdom (fourth century BCE to first century CE). Archaeologist Laila Nehmé recently traveled to Hegra to study its ancient ______ into the rocky outcrops of a vast desert, these burial chambers seem to blend seamlessly with nature.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Hegra is an archaeological site in present-day Saudi Arabia and was the second largest city of the Nabataean Kingdom (fourth century BCE to","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"first century CE). Archaeologist Laila Nehmé recently traveled to Hegra to study its ancient ______ into the rocky outcrops of a vast desert, these","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"burial chambers seem to blend seamlessly with nature.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["tombs. Built","tombs, built","tombs and built","tombs built"]},{"id":"430d929a","domain":"Standard English","skill":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Standard English · Form, Structure, and Sense","passage":"British scientists James Watson and Francis Crick won the Nobel Prize in part for their 1953 paper announcing the double helix structure of DNA, but it is misleading to say that Watson and Crick discovered the double helix. ______ findings were based on a famous X-ray image of DNA fibers, “Photo 51,” developed by X-ray crystallographer Rosalind Franklin and her graduate student Raymond Gosling.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"British scientists James Watson and Francis Crick won the Nobel Prize in part for their 1953 paper announcing the double helix structure of DNA,","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"but it is misleading to say that Watson and Crick discovered the double helix. ______ findings were based on a famous X-ray image of DNA fibers,","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"“Photo 51,” developed by X-ray crystallographer Rosalind Franklin and her graduate student Raymond Gosling.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["They’re","It’s","Their","Its"]},{"id":"3ed5ebb4","domain":"Standard English","skill":"Boundaries","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Standard English · Boundaries","passage":"In her analysis of Edith Wharton’s The House of Mirth (1905), scholar Candace Waid observes that the novel depicts the upper classes of New York society as “consumed by the appetite of a soulless ______ an apt assessment given that The House of Mirth is set during the Gilded Age, a period marked by rapid industrialization, economic greed, and widening wealth disparities.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"In her analysis of Edith Wharton’s The House of Mirth (1905), scholar Candace Waid observes that the novel depicts the upper classes of New","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"York society as “consumed by the appetite of a soulless ______ an apt assessment given that The House of Mirth is set during the Gilded Age, a","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"period marked by rapid industrialization, economic greed, and widening wealth disparities.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["materialism”; and","materialism” and","materialism,”","materialism”"]},{"id":"dd428136","domain":"Standard English","skill":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Standard English · Form, Structure, and Sense","passage":"Cheng Dang and her colleagues at the University of Washington recently ran simulations to determine the extent to which individual snow ______ affect the amount of light reflecting off a snowy surface.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Cheng Dang and her colleagues at the University of Washington recently ran simulations to determine the extent to which individual snow ______","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"affect the amount of light reflecting off a snowy surface.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["grain’s physical properties’","grains’ physical properties","grains’ physical property’s","grains physical properties"]},{"id":"8f6d6ae6","domain":"Standard English","skill":"Boundaries","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Standard English · Boundaries","passage":"Archaeologists have estimated that the pre-Columbian Native American city of Cahokia, located across the Mississippi River from modern-day St. Louis, Missouri, had as many as 20,000 inhabitants in the year 1150 ______ it one of the largest cities in North America at the time.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Archaeologists have estimated that the pre-Columbian Native American city of Cahokia, located across the Mississippi River from modern-day","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"St. Louis, Missouri, had as many as 20,000 inhabitants in the year 1150 ______ it one of the largest cities in North America at the time.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["CE making","CE. Making","CE, making","CE; making"]},{"id":"420dea42","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Transitions","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Expression of Ideas · Transitions","passage":"A blend of gabardine and wool, the material for Elvis Presley’s Gold Vine jumpsuit was flexible enough to allow the singer to perform his signature dance moves. ______ the added weight of the suit’s swirling vines made of gold rhinestones likely limited Elvis’s mobility to some degree.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"A blend of gabardine and wool, the material for Elvis Presley’s Gold Vine jumpsuit was flexible enough to allow the singer to perform his","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"signature dance moves. ______ the added weight of the suit’s swirling vines made of gold rhinestones likely limited Elvis’s mobility to some","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"degree.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","choices":["For this reason,","Firstly,","However,","In other words,"]},{"id":"db3ad406","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Expression of Ideas · Rhetorical Synthesis","passage":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes: Stars form in a galaxy when gravity causes a massive cloud of dust and gas to collapse. A galaxy in a phase of rapid star formation is called a starburst galaxy. Quenching is a process in which a galaxy loses star-forming gas. A galaxy that no longer forms stars is called a quenched galaxy. A quenched galaxy has entered the poststarburst phase. The student wants to explain what a quenched galaxy is.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Stars form in a galaxy when gravity causes a massive cloud of dust and gas to collapse.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"A galaxy in a phase of rapid star formation is called a starburst galaxy.","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Quenching is a process in which a galaxy loses star-forming gas.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"A galaxy that no longer forms stars is called a quenched galaxy.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"A quenched galaxy has entered the poststarburst phase.","indent":2}],"question":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Before quenching, a starburst galaxy will form stars at a rapid rate.","When it becomes quenched, a starburst galaxy enters the poststarburst phase.","Having entered the poststarburst phase, a quenched galaxy is one that no longer forms stars.","A starburst galaxy will lose star-forming gas and eventually become quenched."]},{"id":"4e063114","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Expression of Ideas · Rhetorical Synthesis","passage":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes: Georeferencing is the process of assigning geographic coordinates to an image. This process enables mapping software to place the image in its real-world location. A 2017 project by Tania López Marrero and colleagues georeferenced a set of aerial photographs of Puerto Rico’s coastline taken in 1930. These photographs are the earliest known aerial photographs of Puerto Rico. López Marrero’s project provided data that can help researchers analyze changes in Puerto Rico’s coastline. The student wants to define the term “georeferencing.”","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Georeferencing is the process of assigning geographic coordinates to an image.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"This process enables mapping software to place the image in its real-world location.","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"A 2017 project by Tania López Marrero and colleagues georeferenced a set of aerial photographs of Puerto Rico’s coastline taken in","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"1930.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"These photographs are the earliest known aerial photographs of Puerto Rico.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"López Marrero’s project provided data that can help researchers analyze changes in Puerto Rico’s coastline.","indent":2}],"question":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["A 2017 project by Tania López Marrero and colleagues assigned geographic coordinates to photographs of Puerto Rico’s coastline and also used georeferencing.","Tania López Marrero and colleagues used georeferencing in their analysis of the earliest known aerial photographs of Puerto Rico.","Georeferenced aerial photographs from 1930 can help researchers analyze changes in Puerto Rico’s coastline.","Georeferencing is the process of assigning geographic coordinates to an image so that mapping software can place it in its real-world location."]},{"id":"47e238be","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Transitions","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Expression of Ideas · Transitions","passage":"Seismologists Kaiqing Yuan and Barbara Romanowicz have proposed that the magma fueling Iceland’s more than 30 active volcano systems emerges from deep within Earth. The great depths involved — nearly 3,000 km — mark Iceland’s volcanoes as extreme outliers; ______ many of Earth’s volcanoes are fed by shallow pockets of magma found less than 15 km below the surface.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Seismologists Kaiqing Yuan and Barbara Romanowicz have proposed that the magma fueling Iceland’s more than 30 active volcano systems","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"emerges from deep within Earth. The great depths involved—nearly 3,000 km—mark Iceland’s volcanoes as extreme outliers; ______ many of","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Earth’s volcanoes are fed by shallow pockets of magma found less than 15 km below the surface.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","choices":["indeed,","nevertheless,","in addition,","consequently,"]},{"id":"b88dad9d","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Transitions","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Expression of Ideas · Transitions","passage":"Al-Andalus, the historical region of the Iberian Peninsula that includes most of modern-day Spain, was ruled by various Arabic-speaking Muslim states between the eighth and fifteenth centuries. ______ many Arabic words, such as “alacr án”—meaning “scorpion”—made their way into the Spanish language.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Al-Andalus, the historical region of the Iberian Peninsula that includes most of modern-day Spain, was ruled by various Arabic-speaking Muslim","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"states between the eighth and fifteenth centuries. ______ many Arabic words, such as “alacrán”—meaning “scorpion”—made their way into the","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Spanish language.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","choices":["For example,","Instead,","Specifically,","Consequently,"]},{"id":"9dc4e640","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Transitions","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Expression of Ideas · Transitions","passage":"The mineral mtorolite is most commonly found in Zimbabwe. Mtorolite is cryptocrystalline, meaning that its crystalline structure is so fine that the individual crystals cannot be distinguished by the naked eye or even under a microscope. The crystals in microcrystalline minerals are also not visible to the naked eye; ______ they can usually be seen under a microscope.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"The mineral mtorolite is most commonly found in Zimbabwe. Mtorolite is cryptocrystalline, meaning that its crystalline structure is so fine that","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"the individual crystals cannot be distinguished by the naked eye or even under a microscope. The crystals in microcrystalline minerals are also","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"not visible to the naked eye; ______ they can usually be seen under a microscope.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","choices":["thus,","for example,","that said,","similarly,"]}];
